    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2261ce83_366548cc",
        "filename": "pvmfw/src/exceptions.rs",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 29,
      "author": {
        "id": 1882526
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-09-09T10:28:41Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "It is; In theory, we have 1 GiB of MMIO so sharing \"everything\" wouldn\u0027t be viable. See b/245267332 for a better approach. I think we can still keep it as-is today but know that there\u0027s some tech/security debt, hence merging the TODO.",
      "parentUuid": "67d6ffb4_3e64e6c3",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 29,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 29,
        "endChar": 61
      },
      "revId": "292a7da17358126e0f3f68620a94dcb9fe13bc72",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a2a22877_4d83d6b5",
        "filename": "pvmfw/src/exceptions.rs",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 29,
      "author": {
        "id": 1059815
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-09-09T10:38:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Ah, forgot about that 1GB mapping. So what exactly is the point of the MMIO guard if we\u0027re just going to share almost any page with the host automatically? To me the tech debt isn\u0027t about whether we trigger an exception in stage-1 or 2, but about putting a tighter bound on our MMIO mappings. That in turn would allow us to get rid of issuing the HVCs from exception handlers.",
      "parentUuid": "2261ce83_366548cc",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 29,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 29,
        "endChar": 61
      },
      "revId": "292a7da17358126e0f3f68620a94dcb9fe13bc72",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "96566d3e_d2afdc34",
        "filename": "pvmfw/src/exceptions.rs",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 29,
      "author": {
        "id": 1882526
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-09-09T10:58:53Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Once we have a detailed pVM specification, we can implement (and mandate) this kind of lower-level check, yes. In particular, we should only access MMIO for the UART, and PCI/vsock so could limit the HVC target to those regions; the UART call could even be hardcoded.\n\nBut once we start doing vsock, won\u0027t the device move around dynamically based on the number of vdisks/consoles used by the payload? If that\u0027s the case, we won\u0027t be able to predict it at build time, and it will require discovering where the device is by 1. parsing the DT and 2. querying the bus. In that case, the on-demand approach is neat because we only issue the HVCs for those pages that we actually access.\n\nI might be wrong but I\u0027m under the impression that issuing the HVCs in a stage-1 fault handler will have close to no performance impact as compared with preemptively issuing them at boot, if that\u0027s a worry(?)",
      "parentUuid": "a2a22877_4d83d6b5",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 29,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 29,
        "endChar": 61
      },
      "revId": "292a7da17358126e0f3f68620a94dcb9fe13bc72",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a191250d_fb926e2a",
        "filename": "pvmfw/src/exceptions.rs",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 29,
      "author": {
        "id": 1059815
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-09-09T11:16:24Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "No, the worry is that the MMIO guard becomes useless. It should protect against accidentally/maliciously leaking confidential data via MMIO writes, eg. if you can get pvmfw to overrun a buffer. If at that point pvmfw just automatically disables the guard for that page, then what\u0027s the point?\n\nSo that\u0027s why I\u0027m saying it comes down to creating tighter device mappings. We already create the mappings dynamically. You could completely remove the build-time device mapping, you just won\u0027t get UART until after enabling the dynamically generated page tables (we should do that IMHO). With that you can only map\u0026share the smallest number of pages needed after discovering the devices. Principle of least privilege.",
      "parentUuid": "96566d3e_d2afdc34",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 29,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 29,
        "endChar": 61
      },
      "revId": "292a7da17358126e0f3f68620a94dcb9fe13bc72",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "0e6d5bc5_bb2074db",
        "filename": "pvmfw/src/exceptions.rs",
        "patchSetId": 5
      },
      "lineNbr": 29,
      "author": {
        "id": 1882526
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-09-09T13:43:25Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "\u003e No, the worry is that the MMIO guard becomes useless.\n\nBlindly issuing the HVC does that, yes, but I didn\u0027t pretend otherwise as this pvmfw isn\u0027t supposed to be secure anyway, it only tries to replace the U-Boot one from a functional PoV. In fact, it doesn\u0027t verify the image and even leaves its own secrets behind for the guest to read.\n\nOTOH, validating the HVC target based on pre-existing PTEs does make sense (as long as the mappings can be trusted), irrespective of whether the calls are issued from handlers or not and it sounds like we\u0027re mixing up 2 different questions: the code in this CL isn\u0027t unsecure because it issues the HVC from a handler, it is because it doesn\u0027t validate the target.\n\n\n\u003e With that you can only map\u0026share the smallest number of pages needed after discovering the devices. Principle of least privilege.\n\nAgreed; and that makes much more sense (although slightly more expensive in RAM \u0026 TLBs) than a hard-coded 1GiB mapping. However it doesn\u0027t really address the issue you raised earlier of \"putting a tighter bound on our MMIO mappings\" as we\u0027ve now moved the problem from \"should we share this page?\" to \"should we map this page?\" (which will come naturally once we validate the input DT) and isn\u0027t really an argument against doing the sharing \"on-demand\".\n\n\n\u003e You could completely remove the build-time device mapping\n\nI\u0027m not sure what this brings us given that the UART isn\u0027t discovered anyway (it\u0027s hard-coded in `vmbase`)?",
      "parentUuid": "a191250d_fb926e2a",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 29,
        "startChar": 0,
        "endLine": 29,
        "endChar": 61
      },
      "revId": "292a7da17358126e0f3f68620a94dcb9fe13bc72",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
